.TH MOUTHTYPE-LINUX-SETUP 1 "" "mouthtype-linux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mouthtype-linux-setup \- per-user setup for mouthtype-linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mouthtype-linux-setup
[\fB\-\-skip\-plugin\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-autostart\fR] [\fB\-\-shortcut\fR \fBtoggle\fR|\fBhold\fR]
[\fB\-\-desktop\fR \fIauto|gnome|kde|hyprland\fR] [\fB\-\-grant\fR|\fB\-\-skip\-grant\fR]
.br
.B mouthtype-linux-setup \-\-uninstall
[\fB\-\-purge\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run as a normal user, never with sudo. Checks the required helpers, downloads and verifies the speech model,
runs \fBmouthtype-linux doctor\fR, installs the Omarchy bar plugin when present and enables the
\fBmouthtype-linux\fR systemd user service. \fB\-\-uninstall\fR reverses the per-user pieces and
\fB\-\-purge\fR also deletes models, recordings and configuration.
.PP
On GNOME and KDE (Wayland) the Omarchy plugin is skipped. Setup enables the \fBmouthtype-linux\fR user service,
asks the desktop portal for typing access (a one-time prompt, only on a terminal unless \fB\-\-grant\fR is
given), and adds the settings window to the XDG autostart when it is installed. The dictation shortcut prompt
appears when the service starts. \fB\-\-uninstall\fR removes the service, the autostart entry and the GNOME
fallback shortcut; \fB\-\-purge\fR also removes the stored typing grant.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mouthtype-linux (1)
